<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00BW">Example <span data-tag="00BW">2.1.1.3</span>.</a> Let $M$ be a set, which we regard as a category having only identity morphisms. Then nonunital strict monoidal structures on $M$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="00BU" href="/tag/00BU">2.1.1.1</a>) can be identified with nonunital monoid structures on $M$ (in the sense of Variant <a data-tag="04G3" href="/tag/04G3">1.3.2.8</a>). In particular, any nonunital monoid can be regarded as a nonunital strict monoidal category (having only identity morphisms). </p>
